<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic kidney disease G3b A2 (N18.32)</content>. Confirmed and staged. Race-free eGFR 40 by creatinine and 38 by creatinine with cystatin C, with the earlier value of 59 two years ago showing the disease has been present for at least that long and has fallen by about 10 a year, which is rapid. The older race-adjusted equation would read today&apos;s creatinine as 44 and the earlier one as a normal 64; the race-free result recognizes her disease earlier, as the NKF-ASN Task Force intended. Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 228 mg/g on repeat (240 at intake): A2. Urinalysis from the first visit: 1+ protein, no blood, bland sediment. No monoclonal protein; free light chain ratio normal. Hepatitis B surface antigen, hepatitis C antibody and HIV negative; hepatitis B surface antibody negative. Ultrasound today: kidneys 9.6 and 9.4 cm, both echogenic with thin cortex, no obstruction or stones. Kidney failure risk about 7% at 5 years and 2% at 2 years. Dr. Lindqvist-Moore judges hypertensive kidney disease most likely, with APOL1-associated disease possible. [S]<list><item>Losartan 50 mg daily; potassium and creatinine in two weeks.</item><item>Dapagliflozin to follow once potassium and creatinine are stable.</item><item>Kidney education session and a dietitian session.</item><item>APOL1 testing offered after counseling; she chose to defer it.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hypertension with chronic kidney disease (I12.9)</content>. Home average 150/88 with clinic 154/90: stage 2 hypertension confirmed. Intake ECG showed left ventricular hypertrophy by voltage, consistent with longstanding high blood pressure. [S]<list><item>Losartan as above; goal below 130/80, and a systolic below 120 if tolerated.</item><item>Home readings the week before each visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Anemia of chronic kidney disease (D63.1)</content>. Hemoglobin 11.2 g/dL with ferritin 168 ng/mL and transferrin saturation 24%: iron replete; mild anemia from kidney disease, likely part of her tiredness. No treatment needed at this level. [S]<list><item>Hemoglobin again in three months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin D deficiency with a mildly raised PTH (E55.9)</content>. 25-hydroxyvitamin D 17 ng/mL and PTH 84 pg/mL, with normal calcium and phosphate: correct the vitamin D first. [S]<list><item>Vitamin D3 1,000 units daily; PTH and vitamin D in three months.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Standardized office blood pressure (five minutes&apos; rest, validated cuff, average of two readings)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>154/90</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home blood pressure readings</td><td>Seven days to confirm; then the week before each visit while medicines change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seven-day average 150/88 confirms hypertension</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio</td><td>Repeat to confirm; three to six months after a treatment change; then about twice a year at G3b A2</td><td>Completed today</td><td>228 mg/g, confirming the intake result of 240 (A2)</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Creatinine with cystatin C (eGFRcr-cys)</td><td>When decisions depend on accuracy, as at diagnosis and referral</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Done at intake: eGFR 40 by creatinine, 38 with cystatin C; both race-free</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urinalysis with microscopy</td><td>At diagnosis and with any change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Intake sample: 1+ protein, no blood, bland sediment</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Kidney ultrasound</td><td>Once, at diagnosis of chronic kidney disease of uncertain cause</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Small echogenic kidneys; no obstruction or stones</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Monoclonal protein screen and hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV tests</td><td>Once, for chronic kidney disease of uncertain cause in an older adult</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Drawn at intake: no monoclonal protein, normal free light chain ratio; hepatitis B surface antigen, hepatitis C antibody and HIV negative; not immune to hepatitis B</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Kidney failure risk equation, 2- and 5-year</td><td>At diagnosis and yearly at G3 to G5; before referral decisions</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About 7% at 5 years and 2% at 2 years (eGFR 38, albumin-to-creatinine ratio 240 mg/g, age 68, female)</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Potassium, sodium, bicarbonate, urea nitrogen, creatinine and eGFR</td><td>Two to four weeks after starting or increasing an ARB or SGLT2 inhibitor; then with each eGFR check (about twice a year at G3b A2)</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Losartan started today; check in two weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin</td><td>At least yearly at G3; recheck in three months when mildly low</td><td>Up to date</td><td>11.2 g/dL at intake; iron studies adequate</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Calcium, phosphate, intact PTH and 25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>Baseline at G3; then every 6 to 12 months; vitamin D and PTH three months after starting replacement</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: calcium 9.2, phosphate 3.8 mg/dL; PTH 84 pg/mL; vitamin D 17 ng/mL; vitamin D3 started today</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel</td><td>Once at diagnosis; statin for adults 50 or older with eGFR below 60 without routine repeat testing</td><td>Up to date</td><td>LDL 136 mg/dL at intake; atorvastatin 20 mg started today, without a routine repeat</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication review: NSAIDs, contrast, dose adjustment, sick-day rules</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Napr